    "q-159636": {
      "dom_id": "step-q159636",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "The lengths of traditional baguettes and brown baguettes made at a particular French bakery have standard deviations of $4\\,\\text{cm}$ and $3\\,\\text{cm},$ respectively. The baker wants to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of the lengths of traditional baguettes is larger than the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of the lengths of brown baguettes. He constructs a $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nGiven that the baker sampled $50$ traditional baguettes and $40$ brown baguettes and found that their mean lengths were $65\\,\\text{cm}$ and $63.5\\,\\text{cm},$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $1.5\\pm 1.421.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $90%$ confidence interval are positive.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=65-63.5=1.5\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.05}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.05})=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=1.645:$  \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\n \n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{4}^{2}}{50}+\\frac{{3}^{2}}{40}}≈1.214\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 1.5\\pm 1.214\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The end-points of our confidence interval are $1.5\\pm 1.214.$\n\n- Statement II is true. All values within the confidence interval are positive:\n\n$$\n(1.5-1.214,\\,1.5+1.214)=(0.286,\\,2.714)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since all values within the confidence interval are positive, this suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
